<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klarjeti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Klarjeti is a historic region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the lower Chorokhi valley, today mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artvin">Artvin</a> Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. From the early 9th century it was the base of the Bagratid princes, who rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artanuji">Artanuji</a> and ruled with Byzantine titles, and its monasteries became centres of Georgian learning. Together with Tao it formed Tao-Klarjeti, from which the unified Georgian kingdom grew; it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klarjeti.jpg" alt="Klarjeti" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgi Balakhadze · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meskheti_map_of_VIII-X_centuries_(en).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Klarjeti was traversed by the Chorokhi (Çoruh) and stretched from the Arsiani Range westwards towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shavsheti">Shavsheti</a> and Nigali to the north and by Tao to the south. Its centre was the fortified trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artanuji">Artanuji</a>, now Ardanuç. The region roughly corresponds to the Cholarzene of classical sources and probably to the Kaţarza or Quturza of earlier Urartian records; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> knew it as Kļarjk'. With Tao and several smaller districts it formed the region conventionally called Tao-Klarjeti.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Klarjeti was one of the south-westernmost provinces of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, ruled according to the medieval Georgian chronicles by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnavazid-dynasty">Pharnavazid dynasty</a>. From the 2nd century BCE to the 3rd century CE it was contested between Iberia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and passed back and forth between them. In the division of Iberia between the Roman and Sasanian empires in the 370s it passed to the Roman side, though whether as a province or a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> is unknown. The marriage of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakhtang-i-of-iberia">Vakhtang I of Iberia</a> to the Roman princess Helena seems to have let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> regain it about 485.</p><p>Vakhtang's younger sons and their descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaramid-dynasty">Guaramids</a>, then held Klarjeti and Javakheti until their possessions passed to their Bagrationi cousins. Under the Bagrationi the area prospered: the taxes collected at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artanuji">Artanuji</a> fed their rise, and the land, deserted in an Arab invasion, was repopulated and became a centre of Christian culture through the monastic movement begun by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigol-of-khandzta">Gregory of Khandzta</a> (759–861). Around 870 it became the hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of one branch of the family, the Sovereigns of Klarjeti, until their cousin Bagrat III, the first king of a unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, dispossessed them in 1010 and granted the area to the Abuserisdze family.</p><p>Klarjeti never fully recovered from Seljuk attacks later in the 11th century and declined further during the Mongol invasions and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in the 13th and 14th centuries. After the partition of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the 15th century it passed to the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Meskheti</a>, who lost it to the Ottoman conquest in 1551.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Klarjeti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the south-west the Bagratid princes of Tao-<b>Klarjeti</b> revived the title of king of the Iberians in 888, while in the west the Kingdom of Abkhazia, ruled from Kutaisi, had become a Georgian-speaking Orthodox realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the 9th century the Bagratid prince Ashot I moved from Kartli to the south-western borderland of <b>Klarjeti</b>, where he rebuilt Artanuji and the family won Byzantine titles.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-iberians">Kingdom of the Iberians</a></li></ul></section>
